\code{"base_cols"} attribute that \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}} attaches to
\code{analysis_data}. If \code{analysis_data} has no \code{"base_cols"} attribute (e.g.
it was not built with \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}, or the attribute was
dropped by an intervening subsetting operation), the function stops with
an informative error.

The \code{pcoa_blocks} argument controls which trait sets are analyzed:

\itemize{
\item \code{"all"}: runs PCoA on each individual block in \code{base_cols}
(typically \code{"veg"}, \code{"flo"}, \code{"fru"}), as well as on every pairwise and
full combination of these blocks (\code{"vegflo"}, \code{"vegfru"}, \code{"flofru"},
\code{"vegflofru"});
\item a character vector naming one or more blocks in \code{base_cols}: runs
PCoA only on the requested block(s), individually.
}

For each trait block, rows with missing values in the relevant columns are
removed via \code{\link[stats:na.omit]{stats::na.omit()}}, and traits with zero variance are dropped.
Blocks with fewer than 3 specimens or fewer than 2 traits after this
filtering, or with fewer than 2 ordination axes, are skipped with a
warning.

\strong{Distance.} The distance between specimens is set by \code{dist_method}:

\itemize{
\item \code{"gower"} (default): Gower distance for continuous traits, i.e. the
mean, over traits, of the absolute difference between two specimens
divided by the range of the trait. All traits have the same weight
regardless of their units;
\item \code{"euclidean"}: Euclidean distance on centred and scaled traits. The
resulting PCoA is equivalent to the PCA computed by \code{\link[=morph_pca]{morph_pca()}}.
}

\strong{Ordination.} The PCoA is computed with \code{\link[ape:pcoa]{ape::pcoa()}}. Distances
such as Gower's are not always Euclidean, in which case some eigenvalues are
negative. \code{correction} controls how this is handled: \code{"none"} (default)
uses only the axes with positive eigenvalues; \code{"lingoes"} and \code{"cailliez"}
apply the corresponding additive-constant corrections. The percentage of
variation shown on each axis is its eigenvalue divided by the sum of the
positive eigenvalues (corrected eigenvalues when a correction is used). When
negative eigenvalues are present and \code{correction = "none"}, a message
reports their magnitude.

\strong{Traits.} PCoA is computed from distances, so it has no trait
loadings of its own. As in the usual PCoA biplot, each trait is instead
correlated (\code{\link[stats:cor]{stats::cor()}}) with the ordination axes: the vectors in the
biplot are drawn for the \code{top_vars} traits with the highest squared
correlation with PCo1 and PCo2 combined, with lengths proportional to the
correlations; and the two barplots show the correlation of the \code{top_vars}
traits most correlated with PCo1 and with PCo2, respectively.

Taxon colours are taken from the \code{"taxon_colors"} attribute attached to
\code{analysis_data} by \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}, so that each taxon keeps the
same colour used in other plots (e.g. \code{\link[=morph_pca]{morph_pca()}}, \code{\link[=morph_dapc]{morph_dapc()}}). If
this attribute is missing, or lacks an entry for a taxon present in a given
block, colours are generated automatically from the \code{viridis} palette for
the affected taxa, with a warning.

For each analyzed block, the following figures are generated:

\itemize{
\item a PCoA biplot (specimens and trait vectors) with marginal density
panels along each axis, one density per taxon;
\item a barplot of the correlation of the top traits with PCo1;
\item a barplot of the correlation of the top traits with PCo2;
\item a barplot of the percentage of variation explained by the first
(up to 10) axes;
\item when at least 3 axes are available, a three-panel figure with
PCo1 x PCo2, PCo1 x PCo3, and PCo2 x PCo3 scatterplots.
